systemd: fall back to the frugal fd loop when close_range is missing

With 0008 applied the port reaches multi-user for the first time. Measured on
dandelion (Linux 4.9.190), stage-2 boot of 2026-08-07, read out of the raw log
ring:

  [ 364.120737] systemd[1]: Reached target Login Prompts.
  [ 390.596637] systemd[1]: Reached target Multi-User System.
  [ 390.629646] systemd[1]: Startup finished in 34.899s (kernel) +
                5min 55.724s (userspace) = 6min 30.624s.

No unit ever ran. Every service died between fork() and exec(), 728 times over
the 12 minutes the ring covers:

  Failed to close unwanted file descriptors: Function not implemented
  Failed at step FDS spawning .../tailscaled: Function not implemented
  Failed at step FDS spawning .../sshd: Function not implemented
  Failed at step FDS spawning .../systemd-logind: Function not implemented
  Failed at step FDS spawning .../getty: Function not implemented

The counts are restart loops rather than distinct faults -- dnsmasq alone failed
138 times -- and getty is among the casualties, which is why the device reaches
Login Prompts and still offers no way in.

exec-invoke.c calls close_all_fds() to sanitise the fd table before exec. That
lands in close_all_fds_special_case(), and both it and the sorted loop in
close_all_fds() drive close_range(), which is Linux 5.9. On this kernel the
syscall answers ENOSYS and all three call sites turn that straight into -errno.

systemd already carries the fallback this needs: close_all_fds_frugal() walks the
fd table with close_nointr() and never calls close_range(). close_all_fds() and
close_all_fds_without_malloc() both route to it when the special case reports
"not handled" by returning 0, and it is documented as safe in signal handler
context, so it is safe between fork() and exec() as well.

So report ENOSYS from the special case as "not special cased" rather than as a
failure, and fall back to the frugal loop directly from the sorted path. Any
other errno keeps propagating. A kernel with close_range() never reaches the
added code, and the fallback costs one pass over the fd table -- bounded by
MAX_FD_LOOP_LIMIT, which frugal already enforces.

--- a/src/basic/fd-util.c
+++ b/src/basic/fd-util.c
@@ -303,20 +303,23 @@
 
         case 0:
                 /* Close everything. Yay! */
-                if (close_range(3, INT_MAX, 0) < 0)
-                        return -errno;
+                if (close_range(3, INT_MAX, 0) < 0)
+                        /* close_range() is Linux 5.9. Answering 0 here means "not special cased",
+                         * which sends the caller to close_all_fds_frugal() -- the fallback that is
+                         * already there for the cases this one cannot optimize. */
+                        return errno == ENOSYS ? 0 : -errno;
 
                 return 1;
 
         case 1:
                 /* Close all but exactly one, then we don't need no sorting. This is a pretty common
                  * case, hence let's handle it specially. */
 
-                if (except[0] > 3 && close_range(3, except[0] - 1, 0) < 0)
-                        return -errno;
+                if (except[0] > 3 && close_range(3, except[0] - 1, 0) < 0)
+                        return errno == ENOSYS ? 0 : -errno;
 
-                if (except[0] < INT_MAX && close_range(MAX(3, except[0] + 1), -1, 0) < 0)
-                        return -errno;
+                if (except[0] < INT_MAX && close_range(MAX(3, except[0] + 1), -1, 0) < 0)
+                        return errno == ENOSYS ? 0 : -errno;
 
                 return 1;
 
@@ -391,8 +394,11 @@
                         continue;
 
                 /* Close everything between the start and end fds (both of which shall stay open) */
-                if (close_range(start + 1, end - 1, 0) < 0)
-                        return -errno;
+                if (close_range(start + 1, end - 1, 0) < 0) {
+                        if (errno == ENOSYS)
+                                return close_all_fds_frugal(except, n_except);
+                        return -errno;
+                }
         }
 
         /* The loop succeeded. Let's now close everything beyond the end */
@@ -400,8 +406,11 @@
         if (sorted[n_sorted-1] >= INT_MAX) /* Dont let the addition below overflow */
                 return 0;
 
-        if (close_range(sorted[n_sorted-1] + 1, INT_MAX, 0) < 0)
-                return -errno;
+        if (close_range(sorted[n_sorted-1] + 1, INT_MAX, 0) < 0) {
+                if (errno == ENOSYS)
+                        return close_all_fds_frugal(except, n_except);
+                return -errno;
+        }
 
         return 0;
 }
